<template>
    <heading>Command State Machine</heading>
    <keywords><li>startup</li><li>state machine</li><li>project selection</li></keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p><code>baseOperationsHandler</code> is the initial state. It calls <code>SelectCreateProject</code> for a <code>ModalDialog</code> command naming that dialog, and calls <code>ReloadProject</code> for a <code>ReloadProject</code> command. <code>SelectCreateProject</code> displays the modal in its entry code. When it receives <code>SelectProject</code>, it calls the <code>SelectProject</code> child state. The parent state is suspended during the call, so it keeps the modal open while the child performs selection and loading. If the child returns <code>ProjectLoaded</code>, the parent closes the modal and returns. If it returns <code>ProjectPickerAborted</code>, the parent stays active and the modal remains open for another attempt.</p>
        <p>The <code>SelectProject</code> child starts <code>asyncSelectProject(event)</code> in its entry code, during dispatch of the initiating command. This matters because local selection invokes the browser's directory picker, which needs the click's user activation. While the child is waiting, it handles <code>ProjectLoaded</code> and <code>ProjectPickerAborted</code>; another <code>SelectProject</code> command does not match either handler, so it cannot start a second load. The async function dispatches the <code>ProjectLoaded</code> controller command after loading. Separately, the model emits a <code>ProjectLoaded</code> event that refreshes the view. Project creation is not implemented. This machine uses the controller's supported calls and returns.</p>
        <p>The <code>ReloadProject</code> child starts <code>asyncReloadProject()</code> in its entry code and returns when it receives the <code>ProjectLoaded</code> controller command. Loading also emits the model's <code>ProjectLoaded</code> event, which lets the view refresh. Both child operations use calls and returns, which the controller supports.</p>
        <p>Indentation guide: <code>state</code> starts in column 1, <code>on</code> in column 4, and <code>-&gt;()</code> in column 2. These positions keep the directive structure visually aligned. Indent each directive's JavaScript right-hand side at the current code level, starting in column 7 and adding four columns for each nested JavaScript block. Keep directive tokens in their fixed columns even inside a JavaScript condition. Ordinary JavaScript uses the same indentation as a directive's right-hand side. Indentation has no effect on how directives are parsed.</p>
    </section>
    <section data-lp5-kind="code">
        <name>Command state machine</name>
        <code><![CDATA[
const stateMachineSource = `
state baseOperationsHandler
   on ModalDialog
      if (event.dialogName === "SelectCreateProject") {
 ->()     SelectCreateProject
      }

   on ReloadProject
 ->() ReloadProject

state SelectCreateProject
      view.showModalDialog("SelectCreateProject");

   on SelectProject
 ->() SelectProject
      if (event.name === "ProjectLoaded") {
          view.closeModalDialog("SelectCreateProject");
          return;
      }

state SelectProject
      asyncSelectProject(event);

   on ProjectLoaded
      return;

   on ProjectPickerAborted
      return;

state ReloadProject
      asyncReloadProject();

   on ProjectLoaded
      return;
`;
]]></code></section></template>
